WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:02.010 --> 00:00:06.870
The next few topics will cover unit testing
and how unit testing is done in Python.

00:00:06.870 --> 00:00:12.070
Before we cover unit tests, we need to define
unit testing.

00:00:12.070 --> 00:00:18.100
Unit testing is testing done on a small unit
of code, usually to check the functionality

00:00:18.100 --> 00:00:19.550
of that code.

00:00:19.550 --> 00:00:24.710
For example, we can use a unit test to test
logic within a game, such as whether two values

00:00:24.710 --> 00:00:27.869
that should be equal are equal.

00:00:27.869 --> 00:00:31.490
Running a unit test involves adding code we
need to become familiar with.

00:00:31.490 --> 00:00:36.960
I will go through this code, the 531-unittest
file, from top to bottom.

00:00:36.960 --> 00:00:44.960
First, we need to import the unittest library
to have access to the built-in unittest functions.

00:00:44.960 --> 00:00:47.100
That code is on line 1.

00:00:47.100 --> 00:00:50.070
Unittests are inside classes.

00:00:50.070 --> 00:00:59.660
On line three, we have a class that I named
TestMain that holds a test case as an argument.

00:00:59.660 --> 00:01:01.829
Within the class, a function is defined.

00:01:01.829 --> 00:01:06.210
The function needs to start with the word,
test.

00:01:06.210 --> 00:01:15.189
I named this test test_location as a unit
test for colored locations in a game.

00:01:15.189 --> 00:01:19.549
The self argument indicates a self-contained
unittest.

00:01:19.549 --> 00:01:27.450
I have two values assigned to variables a
and b on line 7 in this test.

00:01:27.450 --> 00:01:30.890
The real test is on line 8.

00:01:30.890 --> 00:01:36.189
This assertEqual test is one of many assertion
tests we can run.

00:01:36.189 --> 00:01:41.610
We will cover assert methods in more detail
in an upcoming video.

00:01:41.610 --> 00:01:45.770
The assertEqual test asserts whether two values
are equal.

00:01:45.770 --> 00:01:53.229
The code on line 10 is an if statement indicating
that, if we are in the app's main file, to

00:01:53.229 --> 00:01:58.100
run the unittest.

00:01:58.100 --> 00:02:06.820
I will run this code, and we get a failure,
as the two values are not equal.

00:02:06.820 --> 00:02:15.190
I will change the b variable to red and run
this code again.

00:02:15.190 --> 00:02:24.140
This time, the test succeeds, as a = b.

00:02:24.140 --> 00:02:27.900
Unit tests are important because they provide
an effective way to test a small amount of

00:02:27.900 --> 00:02:33.230
code, and in doing so, one can more easily
isolate potential problems within an app,

00:02:33.230 --> 00:02:35.640
pinpoint where those problems are, and fix
them.

